<Article slug="operations">
<p>A product page has become slow. The application records a 100 ms Redis call. Is Redis executing a slow command, waiting behind other work, or spending almost all that time outside command execution?</p>
<p>The same elapsed time can arise from each cause. Change the cause below and watch which part of the request grows. The lower bar shows the much smaller question answered by Redis’s slow log: how long did this command take to execute?</p>
<LatencyDiagnosis />
<p>In “Waiting behind another command”, the GET executes for 1 ms but finishes after 100 ms. Its own execution time does not explain the delay. In “Expensive command execution”, almost the whole interval belongs to the command itself. Those cases call for different investigations even though the application sees the same number.</p>
<p>Operating a cache means preserving the application’s latency, correctness and source-capacity contract while the workload and infrastructure change. Redis health is evidence about that contract, not its definition.</p>

<h2>Latency diagnosis: locate the waiting</h2>
<p>Start with the user-visible symptom and its interval. Which operation, deployment cohort, application instance or shard changed? Are requests slow, failing, returning unacceptable data, or loading the source too often? Compare the affected path with an unaffected one before changing configuration.</p>
<p>An application timer around a client call includes more than Redis execution. It can include waiting for a connection, a client queue, transport, Redis scheduling, reply transfer, decoding and time before the application resumes. Instrument useful boundaries and record timeouts separately. A timeout does not tell you whether a command executed, as we saw in <a href="/learn/databases/caching/clients-and-latency/">Redis clients, latency and pipelining</a>.</p>
<p><code>SLOWLOG GET</code> reports commands whose execution exceeded the configured threshold. It excludes network I/O. It does not measure this request’s earlier wait behind another command. A short or absent entry for the affected GET therefore does not establish that Redis was uninvolved. Look for expensive surrounding work, changes in command mix and server latency events as well.</p>
<p>The native lab deliberately sets the slow-log threshold to zero and its maximum length to eight on a private test server. Twelve GETs leave eight retained entries, including command arguments and execution microseconds. This makes the measurement boundary and retention visible; it is not a production threshold recommendation or a benchmark.</p>
<p>Use bounded captures. Slow logs can contain keys and arguments; access to them should be controlled accordingly. Redis’s latency monitor can record server events above a configured threshold, while application traces supply the experience at the caller. Neither replaces the other. Preserve the relevant evidence before a restart or configuration change erases the context.</p>
<p>A diagnosis should connect observations. Rising command execution time alongside a larger response suggests different work from normal execution with growing client queues. Rising evictions, more misses and increased source loads suggest a capacity or working-set change. Authentication errors following a credential rollout suggest a different boundary again. These are hypotheses to test, not unique signatures.</p>
<p>Check meanings before interpreting reassuring names. For example, <code>blocked_clients</code> counts clients waiting on blocking commands such as BLPOP; it is not a count of all requests waiting for Redis execution. Host memory, Redis allocation and the eviction limit also measure different things, as <a href="/learn/databases/caching/memory-and-performance/">Redis memory and performance</a> explains.</p>

<h2>Hit rates, counter deltas and application outcomes</h2>
<p>A long history of successful hits can hide a new failure. Suppose the first sample contains 900 hits and 100 misses. By the next sample, the counters have reached 920 and 180. The lifetime hit rate still looks respectable. The recent interval tells a different story.</p>
<CounterWindow />
<p>Only twenty of the hundred new lookups hit. The source may now be receiving much more work, even though the cumulative ratio is 83.6%. Compute rates from counter differences over the same time window, and divide by the elapsed time when you need lookups per second.</p>
<p>Now choose “Counters reset”. Subtracting the earlier sample produces negative counts. The lab executes <code>CONFIG RESETSTAT</code> on its private instance and observes both hit and miss counters return to zero while <code>run_id</code> stays the same. A restart is not the only way continuity can break. Detect counter decreases, process changes and missing samples; establish a new baseline rather than reporting a negative rate. Do not reset production statistics merely to obtain a measurement window.</p>
<p>With no new lookups, the interval has no hit-rate estimate. Report that absence rather than inventing 0% or 100%. Across shards, add compatible hit and miss deltas before computing the ratio. Averaging a quiet shard’s percentage with a busy shard’s percentage gives them equal weight despite different traffic.</p>
<p>Redis’s <code>keyspace_hits</code> and <code>keyspace_misses</code> count key lookups, not successful business requests. Commands can touch several keys; L1 hits do not reach Redis; a returned object can fail the application’s age or schema check. Connection failures are another outcome, not ordinary misses.</p>
<p>Record the application path explicitly: requests, accepted local hits, accepted Redis hits, absent or rejected entries, Redis errors, admitted source loads, refused work and final outcomes. Measure latency distributions alongside traffic and errors. Keep labels bounded—operation, cache namespace, deployment cohort and shard are usually more useful than one time series per raw key or user.</p>
<p>Alerts should identify a threatened contract and a useful response. Source load approaching its tested budget, excessive end-to-end latency, or an invalidation backlog exceeding the freshness allowance can be actionable. A hit-rate change alone may simply reflect the successful introduction of L1. Read the denominator before paging someone.</p>

<h2>Redis security and access control lists</h2>
<p>A cache can contain private data and enough authority to disrupt the application. Keep Redis reachable only from the application and operational networks that need it. End-user browsers should reach the application, which mediates Redis access. Protected mode is a useful safeguard; it is not a substitute for deliberate network boundaries.</p>
<p>Authentication identifies the connection. Transport encryption protects data in transit. Authorisation limits what that identity may do. Configure TLS where traffic crosses a network, verify the server’s certificate and name, and account for replication and Cluster links as well as application connections. A password sent over an unencrypted connection does not encrypt the session.</p>
<p>Redis <strong>access control lists</strong>, or <strong>ACLs</strong>, can restrict both commands and key patterns. The lab gives a cache application permission for its catalogue namespace and a small command set. Try reading an orders key, then try FLUSHALL.</p>
<CacheAccess />
<p>The orders read fails even though GET is allowed. FLUSHALL fails because the command is not allowed; a catalogue prefix does not constrain the reach of a command which operates on the whole database. Application credentials should not carry broad administrative permissions merely because they are convenient during development.</p>
<p>The lab starts from a reset policy, explicitly allows GET, SET, DEL and PING, and limits keys to <code>cache:catalog:*</code>. It also permits the pinned client’s <code>CLIENT SETINFO</code> handshake subcommand. This is an example for this client and workload, not a universal policy. Tracking, scripts, transactions, Cluster discovery or another client may require different permissions. Test startup, normal work and reconnect with the actual restricted identity.</p>
<p>The native tests verify that catalogue GET and SET succeed while an orders read, CONFIG, FLUSHALL and SLOWLOG fail with NOPERM. The rejected FLUSHALL leaves the test value intact. The administrative connection can inspect ACL log entries distinguishing key and command denials. An <code>ACL DRYRUN</code> check permits a proposed catalogue SET without executing it: the value remains 12.</p>
<p>Dry-run permission checks are useful before a change, but do not prove the application’s complete connection path works. Keep the administrative identity separate, review the effective policy, and persist it through the deployment’s configuration, ACL-file or managed-service workflow. Repeated <code>ACL SETUSER</code> calls modify existing rules incrementally unless you explicitly reset the policy; adding a narrower-looking rule does not automatically remove old permissions.</p>
<p>An ACL key pattern also does not know which end user owns an object. A catalogue-wide application identity can access every matching key. The application must still enforce tenant and user permissions, construct safe keys and bound request sizes and work. ACLs limit access; they do not provide a per-tenant capacity guarantee.</p>

<h2>Credential rotation and connection revocation</h2>
<p>Store credentials in the deployment’s secret-management system and avoid exposing them in command history, URLs, metrics or logs. Rotation is a change to a running fleet, not just an edit to a password field.</p>
<p>For a planned rotation, Redis supports overlapping valid passwords. Add the new credential, deploy clients which use it, verify that new connections work, then retire the old credential once the migration is established. Include connection pools and rarely restarted workers in that verification. Ensure the durable configuration reflects the intended result.</p>
<p>The lab shows why new authentication and existing sessions must be considered separately. Turning the user off rejects fresh authentication, but an already authenticated connection still reads its allowed key. Removing the old password similarly prevents a fresh login with that password while the existing session continues to work.</p>
<p>If an incident requires active sessions to be revoked, changing credentials alone is insufficient. Use the appropriate connection-revocation mechanism as well. The lab’s administrative connection runs <code>CLIENT KILL USER cache-app</code> against only its two owned application sessions, observes both close, then reconnects using the new credential. A production revocation can clear local caches, trigger reconnects and increase fallback traffic; its runbook must account for that load while restoring the access boundary.</p>
<p>These tests use generated, temporary credentials on a private Unix socket. They establish ACL and session behaviour in Redis 8.2.2. They do not test TLS, a provider’s identity integration or your production network policy.</p>

<h2>Canary deployments and cold-cache capacity</h2>
<p>Changing a key prefix can make an otherwise healthy cache cold. A new schema version, cluster migration, rolling process restart or local-cache flush can have a similar effect. Plan the transition using the source’s available capacity, not only Redis’s spare memory.</p>
<p>The next model sends a fraction of traffic to a new namespace. The old namespace still hits 95% of the time. Initially the new one has no useful entries. Move the traffic share from 20% down to 10%, then consider a cohort which has established 90% hits.</p>
<CacheRollout />
<p>At 20% cold traffic, the old path causes 40 source loads per second and the new path causes 200. Their total exceeds the assumed budget of 200. At 10%, demand is 45 plus 100: 145. The relevant quantity is the combined load, including traffic which has not moved yet.</p>
<p>A <strong>canary deployment</strong> exposes a bounded cohort before expanding the change. Observe that cohort’s correctness, hit rate, latency, failures and source work separately. Increase traffic only after those measurements support the next step. Keep admission control in place: a forecast does not protect the database if the forecast is wrong.</p>
<p>Warming also consumes capacity. Preloading values adds source work and Redis writes, and can evict useful entries. Coexisting namespaces duplicate some data. Include that overlap in the memory budget and keep old and new representations safe during mixed-version operation. A warm cache is not useful if its contents violate the new application contract.</p>
<p>Define rollback before rollout. If old entries stopped receiving invalidations during the transition, routing back to the old prefix can revive stale data. Retain the necessary invalidation coverage, validate age or version, or deliberately discard and refill the old path within a bounded budget. “Change the prefix back” is not, by itself, a correctness argument.</p>
<p>For Redis or client upgrades, exercise the exact command set, permissions, scripts, topology discovery and reconnect behaviour in a representative environment. Check release-specific compatibility and provider procedures. Test recovery with the chosen persistence and replica policy, including what happens to source load and stale entries. The <a href="/learn/databases/caching/replication-and-failover/">recovery article</a> explains why a successful restore can still restore an old answer.</p>

<h2>An incident runbook that preserves the contract</h2>
<p>A useful runbook names an owner, a symptom, the evidence to collect, the scope of an intervention, its stopping condition and its recovery checks. It should be executable by someone who did not design the cache.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Establish impact.</strong> Identify affected operations, cohorts and times. Check errors, unacceptable data and source pressure as well as latency.</li>
<li><strong>Contain amplification.</strong> Keep source admission and retry limits active. If bypassing Redis, calculate and bound the resulting source work; do not assume every missed cache request can be served elsewhere.</li>
<li><strong>Locate the changed boundary.</strong> Compare application traces, recent counter deltas, client connections, command execution, memory, topology and recent changes. Capture enough evidence to distinguish competing explanations.</li>
<li><strong>Make a bounded change.</strong> Choose an intervention with an explicit scope and rollback path. Observe its effect on user outcomes and source load before expanding it.</li>
<li><strong>Verify recovery.</strong> Check freshness, normal and failure paths, reconnects and refill load. A responding Redis process is only one part of a recovered application.</li>
</ol>
<p>A blanket flush or restart can remove evidence and turn a local problem into a cold-cache event. Use one only when its expected effect, scope and refill consequences address the diagnosed problem. Rehearse the runbook with a controlled failure and compare the expected observations with what actually happened.</p>
